   "id": "us-cpi-fed-hike",
   "group": "United States",
   "headline": "Hot October inflation and Fed speakers point to another rate rise next month",
   "when": "Fri 13 Nov · US close",
   "what": "US consumer prices rose 0.4% in October, above the 0.3% forecast. Core prices rose 0.3% against 0.2% expected. Energy, shelter and tariff-hit goods all contributed. Fed governor Waller leans to a quarter-point hike on 9 December and has not ruled out half a point. Boston Fed president Collins called a steady, gradual path of hikes appropriate. The S&P 500 closed down 0.40% at 7,561.67, the Nasdaq down 0.91%, and the 10-year yield rose 5 bp to 5.15%.",
   "why": "Higher rates weigh on large US stocks and on the S&P 500 feeder, your biggest holding at 14.1%. They also pull on growth names such as Apple and Meta. A stronger dollar tends to hit crypto and Asian equities. The US funds are priced at 12 November, so Friday's fall is not yet in them.",
